@@ -137,6 +137,65 @@ to indicates which is the bigger version:
 * ``1`` if the first version is greater than the second version.
 
 
+pysemver sort
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+Sort versions, oldest first.
+
+.. code:: bash
+
+   pysemver sort [<VERSION>...]
+
+.. option:: <VERSION>
+
+    Versions to sort. If none are given, versions are read from
+    *standard input*, one per line; blank lines are ignored.
+
+The sorted versions are printed on *standard out* as plain text,
+one version per line, which makes the output easy to consume
+in a shell loop::
+
+    $ pysemver sort 1.10.0 1.2.0 1.2.0-rc.1
+    1.2.0-rc.1
+    1.2.0
+    1.10.0
+    $ pysemver sort 1.10.0 1.2.0 | while read v; do echo "$v"; done
+    1.2.0
+    1.10.0
+    $ printf '1.10.0\n\n1.2.0\n' | pysemver sort
+    1.2.0
+    1.10.0
+
+If any argument (or input line) is not a valid version, nothing is printed on
+standard out, an error message is printed on standard error, and the
+return code is 2.
+
+
+pysemver latest
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+Print the highest version that is not a prerelease.
+
+.. code:: bash
+
+   pysemver latest [<VERSION>...]
+
+.. option:: <VERSION>
+
+    Versions to choose from. If none are given, versions are read from
+    *standard input*, one per line; blank lines are ignored.
+
+The result is printed on *standard out* as a JSON string, so it can be
+parsed with ``fromJSON()`` or ``jq``::
+
+    $ pysemver latest 1.10.0 1.2.0 2.0.0-rc.1
+    "1.10.0"
+
+If any argument (or input line) is not a valid version, or if there is no
+non-prerelease version, nothing is printed on standard out, an error message
+is printed on standard error, and the return code is 2.
+
+
 Return Code
 -----------
 
